# Approach 3 — Optimal (Bottom-Up 3D DP)

---

## Intuition

Convert recursion into iteration.

Instead of solving top-down,

fill answers from last index.

---

## DP Definition

```
dp[i][first][second]
```

Means

```
Starting from index i

Current gcd of first

Current gcd of second

Number of valid ways
```

---

## Base Case

At

```
i=n
```

Only one state valid

```
first==second

and

both non-zero
```

---

## Transition

```
Skip
```

```
dp[i+1][first][second]
```

---

```
Take First
```

```
dp[i+1][gcd(first,num)][second]
```

---

```
Take Second
```

```
dp[i+1][first][gcd(second,num)]
```

---

## Python Code

```python
from math import gcd

class Solution:
    def subsequencePairCount(self, nums):
        MOD = 10**9 + 7
        n = len(nums)
        maxEl = max(nums)

        dp = [[[0]*(maxEl+1) for _ in range(maxEl+1)] for _ in range(n+1)]

        for first in range(maxEl+1):
            for second in range(maxEl+1):
                if first != 0 and second != 0 and first == second:
                    dp[n][first][second] = 1

        for i in range(n-1,-1,-1):

            for first in range(maxEl+1):

                for second in range(maxEl+1):

                    skip = dp[i+1][first][second]

                    take1 = dp[i+1][gcd(first,nums[i])][second]

                    take2 = dp[i+1][first][gcd(second,nums[i])]

                    dp[i][first][second] = (skip+take1+take2)%MOD

        return dp[0][0][0]
```

